@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+# ATTa v121.3: stop, record, one bounded fix, retest, with no blind retries
+
+Built on v121.2 (isolated parallel jobs). Every app still runs as its own job in parallel. v121.3 changes
+what happens when one of them fails:
+
+```
+TEST -> FAIL -> STOP THAT APP -> RECORD EXACTLY WHERE -> CLASSIFY -> ONE BOUNDED FIX -> RETEST THE FAILED STAGE
+     -> REGRESSION -> CONTINUE -> FINAL VERIFICATION
+```
+
+## Per app (`app_runner._repair_cycle`)
+
+1. **The app stops at its first failed stage.** The watcher already stops at the first FAIL, and later stages are marked SKIPPED.
+
+2. **Where it failed is recorded, append-only**, in `state/runner/failures/<app>/<run>-a<attempt>-f<n>.json`. The record holds:
+   - app, run, attempt, repair number, correlation ID, deployment and app revision, timestamp
+   - stage and code
+   - exact location: for a start failure, the way of running it, the attempt and the rule; for a browser failure, the URL and journey step
+   - the error, the start attempts and their evidence, and the stage-6 evidence folder
+   - the container log, captured while the app is still up (`.container.log`)
+   - the likely class and what needs to change, from a table (`resilience/failures.py`); an uncovered failure is HUMAN_REVIEW with "evidence insufficient"
+   - the retest plan
+
+   Records are never overwritten.
+
+3. **One bounded fix comes from ATTa's own repair tiers,** cheapest first:
+   - tier 1: known fixes (built-in and learned rules keyed on the failure)
+   - tier 2: the capability adapter, which repairs only what its inspection actually finds
+
+   The self-healer's blind probe is **not** used. The same fix is never applied twice to the same failure.
+
+4. **The app stops without a retest when:**
+   - no evidence-based fix exists: **NO_MATCHING_REPAIR**, reported for the next repair decision
+   - a known rule says a person is needed: **NEEDS_HUMAN**
+   - the fix changed nothing it could affect (same app files, registration, proxy and browser state): **FIX_CHANGED_NOTHING**. Retesting would repeat an unchanged test.
+
+5. **Retest the failed stage.** If the fix did not change how the app starts, the app stays up. The watcher walks stages 1 → 6 again on the running app, so the earlier stages are the regression check. If the fix changed how it starts, the app is restarted through the normal runner. Each retest has its own correlation ID, its own revision snapshot and its own evidence folder (`…-r<n>`).
+
+6. **Compare.** Retest results:
+   - same failure (same stage, code and normalised detail): **FIX_DID_NOT_RESOLVE**, and the app stops. Both failures are kept, and a learned rule that was applied takes a FAILED.
+   - passes every stage: **RESOLVED**, then the final verification barrier.
+   - a different failure: **PROGRESSED**. It is recorded and gets its own bounded fix, at most `APP_BUILDER_REPAIR_CYCLES` (default 3) times.
+
+7. **The barrier verifies against the revision the final check ran on.** A repair that changed the app is on its cycle record; the retest after it is the check.
+
+## Blind retries removed
+
+| Where | v121 / v121.2 | v121.3 |
+|---|---|---|
+| Start loop (`run_app`) | A fix of the same kind could be retried on the same failure (up to 3 port clashes, rate-limit waits) | The same rule with the same evidence right after a fix gives FIX_DID_NOT_RESOLVE for that way of running it |
+| Warm-up rechecks (5xx) | Up to 3 identical re-runs after 60 s waits | One wait. Another only if the failure changed (the app is progressing) |
+| Self-healer (`maintenance.heal`, qualify layer) | Tiers 1-2 again, then the probe (general repairs no evidence points to), then a full-catalogue re-run, for up to 8 rounds | Items already repaired in-job skip tiers 1-2. There is no probe; the LLM tier runs only if configured; otherwise the item goes straight to a person. No catalogue re-run unless something was actually applied |
+
+## Regression
+
+- **App-scoped repairs** (chmod, overlay, registration, proxy): the retest re-checks every stage of that app.
+- **Repairs that aren't limited to one app** (`install_browser`, `add_runner_rule`): at the end of the run, every app that qualified before the repair is checked again. A change of verdict is recorded as a `REGRESSION` event.
+
+## Failure report
+
+`state/runner/RUN-REPORT.md` has a "Failure report" section, and `RUN-REPORT.json` has `failure_report`. Each entry gives:
+
+- app
+- failed stage
+- exact location
+- error/output
+- logs and evidence (paths)
+- likely classification
+- what needs to change
+- what to retest
+- every repair tried, with its outcome and retest result
+- final state
+
+An app that was repaired still shows its original failure.
+
+## Validation
+
+- Python: 154 passed, 4 skipped. That includes the 12 new tests in `tests/test_v121_3_stop_fix_retest.py`, which use the real `repair_actions`, known fixes, adapter, maintenance and report.
+- Mutation check: 8 of the new behaviours were reverted one at a time, and a test failed every time.
